IMPORT prelude

-- Missions Etrangeres Ordinance 1891 (Singapore) -- who "The Procureur in Singapore of La Societe des Missions Etrangeres" is and when
-- a Procureur is "duly qualified" (s 2(1), (4)), what a Gazette notification proves (s 2(5)), what the Ordinance empowers the
-- Corporation to do (s 2(2), (3)), when its deeds are duly sealed and what the signature proves (s 3), and whose rights are saved (s 4).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/MEO1891.txt. The deposit says the edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021". The only amendment
-- annotation in the body is "[7/97]" under s 4. The Ordinance is dated 13 November 1891. (Accents are dropped from the Society's name
-- in identifiers and comments here; the deposit spells it "Missions Étrangères".)
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0239 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: tier 4 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by everyday-life
-- relevance. It asks what the Ordinance decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper question yet.
-- The Ordinance has four sections, so the whole of it except the short title is in scope. For a person outside the Society it matters
-- when buying, leasing or lending against the Corporation's property: whether the person executing is a duly qualified Procureur
-- (s 2(4)) and whether the deed is duly sealed (s 3).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 1 (short title); the preamble (recitals, not enacting words); the content of s 48 of the Conveyancing and Law of
-- Property Act 1886, to which ss 2(4) and 3(1) refer (whether an instrument was "filed" or "registered" under it is taken as a stated
-- fact); how the Society itself appoints its Procureur, about which the Ordinance says nothing.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Missions Etrangeres Ordinance 1891 -- the Corporation`

§§ `2(1), 2(4) and 2(5) -- who is the Corporation, and what the Gazette proves`

-- s 2(1): Nicolas Justin Couvreur "and his successors for the time being in the office of Procureur at Singapore of 'La Societe des
-- Missions Etrangeres', duly qualified as hereinafter provided, shall be a body corporate, hereinafter called the Corporation".
-- s 2(4): "No person shall be deemed to be duly qualified as aforesaid unless and until he has first, with the approval of the Minister
-- signified under his hand and seal, caused the power of attorney or other instrument constituting him such Procureur to be duly filed
-- in the Registry of the Supreme Court at Singapore, pursuant to section 48 of the Conveyancing and Law of Property Act 1886, and
-- unless and until a notification of such filing has appeared in the Gazette."
-- s 2(5): "Such notification shall be sufficient evidence of the appointment, and that the person therein named is duly qualified as
-- by this Ordinance is required."
--
-- Four things make a duly qualified Procureur: holding the office for the time being, the filing of the constituting instrument in the
-- Registry under s 48, the Minister's approval of that filing under hand and seal, and a Gazette notification of the filing. The
-- Gazette notification is both the last condition of qualification and "sufficient evidence" of it; the text says "sufficient", not
-- "conclusive", so a notification of a filing made without the Minister's approval is evidence of qualification though, on the
-- encoded conditions, the person is not qualified. Whether such evidence can be rebutted is not modelled.
DECLARE Claimant HAS
    `holds the office of Procureur at Singapore for the time being` IS A BOOLEAN
    `constituting instrument is filed in the Supreme Court Registry under section 48` IS A BOOLEAN
    `filing was approved by the Minister under hand and seal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `notification of the filing has appeared in the Gazette` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the claimant` IS A Claimant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a duly qualified Procureur` IF
        `the claimant`'s `holds the office of Procureur at Singapore for the time being`
    AND `the claimant`'s `constituting instrument is filed in the Supreme Court Registry under section 48`
    AND `the claimant`'s `filing was approved by the Minister under hand and seal`
    AND `the claimant`'s `notification of the filing has appeared in the Gazette`

GIVEN `the claimant` IS A Claimant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Gazette is sufficient evidence of appointment and qualification` IF
    `the claimant`'s `notification of the filing has appeared in the Gazette`

§§ `2(2) and 2(3) -- what the Ordinance empowers the Corporation to do`

-- s 2(1): the Corporation has perpetual succession "and shall and may have and use a corporate seal".
-- s 2(2): "The said seal may from time to time be broken, changed, altered and made anew as the Corporation thinks fit."
-- s 2(3): "The Corporation may acquire, purchase, take, hold and enjoy movable and immovable property of every description, and may
-- sell, convey, assign, surrender and yield up, mortgage, demise, re-assign, transfer or otherwise dispose of any movable and immovable
-- property vested in the Corporation upon such terms as the Corporation thinks fit."
--
-- The powers of disposal reach only property "vested in the Corporation". The Ordinance says nothing about suing or being sued, or
-- about borrowing other than by mortgage; those return FALSE here as powers this Ordinance grants (whether the general law supplies them
-- is outside the text and not encoded).
DECLARE Power IS ONE OF
    `use and change its corporate seal`
    `acquire or purchase property`
    `hold and enjoy property`
    `sell property`
    `mortgage property`
    `lease property`
    `transfer or otherwise dispose of property`
    `sue or be sued`
    `borrow money without security`

GIVEN `the power` IS A Power, `the property is vested in the Corporation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Ordinance empowers the Corporation to` IF
    CONSIDER `the power`
    WHEN `use and change its corporate seal` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `acquire or purchase property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `hold and enjoy property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `sell property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation`
    WHEN `mortgage property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation`
    WHEN `lease property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation`
    WHEN `transfer or otherwise dispose of property` THEN `the property is vested in the Corporation`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `3 -- sealing and signing the Corporation's instruments`

-- s 3(1): "No deed, document or other instrument, sealed with the seal of the Corporation, shall be deemed to be duly sealed unless such
-- seal has been affixed in the presence of the Procureur duly qualified as aforesaid, or his attorney duly authorised by a power of
-- attorney registered under section 48 of the Conveyancing and Law of Property Act 1886, and unless such deed, document or other
-- instrument is signed by the Procureur or his attorney."
-- s 3(2): "Such signing shall be sufficient evidence of the due sealing of such deed, document or other instrument."
--
-- Three things must all be true: the seal, its affixing in the presence of the duly qualified Procureur or a registered attorney, and
-- the signature of the Procureur or his attorney. "The Procureur" in the signing limb is read as the duly qualified Procureur of the
-- presence limb, and "his attorney" as the registered attorney (an inference from "as aforesaid" and the definite article; the signing
-- limb does not repeat the qualifications). Witness and signer need not be the same person on the words of s 3(1); that reading is
-- encoded. s 2(4) speaks of the Procureur's own instrument being "filed", s 3(1) of the attorney's power being "registered".
DECLARE Officer IS ONE OF
    `the duly qualified Procureur`
    `a Procureur not yet duly qualified`
    `an attorney under a registered power of attorney`
    `an attorney whose power of attorney is not registered`
    `another member of the Society`
    `nobody`

DECLARE Instrument HAS
    `is sealed with the Corporation seal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `sealed in the presence of` IS AN Officer
    `signed by` IS AN Officer

GIVEN `the officer` IS AN Officer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the qualified Procureur or a registered attorney` IF
    CONSIDER `the officer`
    WHEN `the duly qualified Procureur` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an attorney under a registered power of attorney` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the instrument` IS AN Instrument
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is duly sealed under section 3` IF
        `the instrument`'s `is sealed with the Corporation seal`
    AND `is the qualified Procureur or a registered attorney` (`the instrument`'s `sealed in the presence of`)
    AND `is the qualified Procureur or a registered attorney` (`the instrument`'s `signed by`)

GIVEN `the instrument` IS AN Instrument
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the signature is sufficient evidence of due sealing` IF
        `the instrument`'s `is sealed with the Corporation seal`
    AND `is the qualified Procureur or a registered attorney` (`the instrument`'s `signed by`)

§§ `4 -- whose rights are saved`

-- s 4: "Nothing in this Ordinance shall affect the rights of the Government."
--
-- Only the Government is named. A FALSE here means only that s 4 does not save the person's rights; it does not mean the Ordinance
-- affects them.
GIVEN `is the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 4 saves their rights` IF
    `is the Government`
